  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.bootstrap` has one MUST. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under the cited parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, a home states its topic in full, and exactly one skill does so. The parent also defines a skill as its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` is marked `{test}`. It covers the obligation in two parts.\n\n1. **The skill states the topic.**\n   - The test reads the skill's `SKILL.md` through `skill_md`. It requires the sections `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. The `section` helper asserts that each heading exists and returns the body.\n   - It asserts specific content phrases in those sections:\n     - Proposing: \"Write one requirement per obligation a user could rely on\" and \"Label each proposal and act on the label\".\n     - Tagging: \"`{test}` on tests and `{impl}` on roots\" and \"Map each other inventoried item to the requirement whose root reaches it\".\n     - Report: the fields `Tests mapped:`, `Items mapped:`, `Independent recount:` and `Gaps:`.\n   - These phrases cover the derivation workflow from inventory through proposal and tagging to the report.\n\n2. **Only this skill states it.** `assert_home(\"habeas-bootstrap-requirements\")` does two things.\n   - It asserts that the skill's text contains every marker from `home_markers`. These markers are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\".\n   - It asserts that none of the other four skills in `SKILLS` contains any of those markers. Each other skill is checked as `bundled_skill`, meaning its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files, collapsed. A restatement elsewhere would therefore fail the test. This covers the \"exactly one skill\" half of the home notion.\n\n**Counterexamples.**\n- If the bootstrap skill lost one of its sections, a key phrase, or a report field, the test would fail.\n- If another skill, or one of its reference files, restated a bootstrap marker, `assert_home` would fail.\n\nI found no input class or condition the requirement names that the test leaves unexercised. The checks are phrase-based, which is the natural way to test prose skill content. The test has no `{fixme}` acknowledgments and needs none.",
  "references": "b02e72a8f64a2f9a1f68893267db6677c2ecf07ab8202182d024a68119169ae3",
